---
/**
 * "Personal Jarvis is now Dodaios" — what the notice bar links to.
 *
 * Two jobs. It tells someone who arrived with the old name that they are in
 * the right place, and it tells someone with an old install the one thing
 * they have to do by hand: the app looked for updates at the repository's old
 * address, so releases up to `LAST_OLD_UPDATER_VERSION` do not see newer ones.
 *
 * Every statement is checkable. The update check of 3.1.1 is
 * `_fetch_latest_release` in the app's `dodaios/ui/web/update_routes.py`: it
 * asks api.github.com for the latest release under the old repository name,
 * GitHub answers 301 since the move, and the check treats anything but 200 as
 * "no update". The install lines come from
 * `src/lib/install.ts`, which holds them verbatim from the app's README, and
 * the README is also the source for "run it again and it updates your
 * existing installation". Nothing here promises a date or a version that does
 * not exist yet.
 *
 * It borrows the legal pages' frame for its typography: one bounded column of
 * prose with the site's chrome and footer.
 *
 * An inline element or expression never starts a source line here: the build's HTML
 * compression drops the line break in front of one, and the space with it.
 */
import LegalPage from "@/components/LegalPage.astro";
import { INSTALL_COMMANDS, RELEASES, REPO } from "@/lib/install";
import { FORMER_NAME, LAST_OLD_UPDATER_VERSION } from "@/lib/rename";
---

<LegalPage
  title={`${FORMER_NAME} is now Dodaios — Dodaios`}
  description={`${FORMER_NAME} has a new name, Dodaios, and a new address. Same project and team. If you installed version ${LAST_OLD_UPDATER_VERSION} or older, update once by hand.`}
  heading={`${FORMER_NAME} is now Dodaios`}
  dateline="October 2026"
>
  <p>
    {FORMER_NAME} has a new name: <strong>Dodaios</strong>. It is the same
    project, built by the same team. Only the name and the addresses changed.
  </p>

  <h2>What changed</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The name. {FORMER_NAME} is now Dodaios.</li>
    <li>The website. It is now <a href="/">dodaios.ai</a>.</li>
    <li>
      The source code. It is now at <a href={REPO}>github.com/Dodaios/Dodaios</a>.
      Links to the old repository on GitHub redirect there.
    </li>
    <li>The logo. It is now a small hooded figure.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What did not change</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The app and what it does.</li>
    <li>
      The licence. Dodaios is free and open source under
      the <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/LICENSE`}>Apache License 2.0</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Your settings, API keys and data. They stay on your computer, where
      they were.
    </li>
    <li>
      A few names you type or see on your disk. The command is
      still <code>jarvis</code>, the settings file is still <code>jarvis.toml</code>,
      the Python package is still <code>personal-jarvis</code>, and the
      installer files still start with <code>PersonalJarvis</code>. They all
      work as they are. Newer versions add a <code>dodaios</code> command
      (and <code>dodaiosctl</code>), which is now the one to use;
      <code>jarvis</code> keeps working.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="update">If you already have it installed: update once</h2>
  <p>
    Versions up to and including {LAST_OLD_UPDATER_VERSION} ask for updates
    at the repository's old address. Since the move that address only answers
    with a pointer to the new one, which those versions do not follow, so the
    app will not offer you an update by itself. Update once by hand, in one of
    two ways. Your settings, API keys and data stay.
  </p>

  <h3>Run the install command again</h3>
  <p>
    The installer notices the existing installation and updates it in place.
  </p>
  <p>macOS and Linux, in a terminal:</p>
  <pre><code>{INSTALL_COMMANDS.unix}</code></pre>
  <p>Windows, in PowerShell:</p>
  <pre><code>{INSTALL_COMMANDS.windows}</code></pre>

  <h3>Or download a newer installer</h3>
  <p>
    If you installed the desktop app from a download, get the installer for
    your system from the <a href={RELEASES}>release page</a> and install it. It
    has to be a release newer than {LAST_OLD_UPDATER_VERSION}, because that
    version itself still looks at the old address.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <p>
    If something does not work after the update, open an issue in
    the <a href={`${REPO}/issues`}>repository</a> or ask
    on <a href="https://discord.gg/x7USduHxbc">Discord</a>.
  </p>
</LegalPage>
